"None of this claims that a headache location has only one possible cause, or that this reading replaces an actual examination. What it shows is that \"referred pain\" and \"location-specific symptoms\" are not exotic ideas unique to Chinese medicine — they are a shared observation that two very different medical traditions arrived at through different methods, and meridians are simply the map Chinese medicine built to organize that observation systematically.": "80200eefe507",
"One of the clearest ways to make meridians feel less abstract is to connect them to something Western medicine already accepts: referred pain. A heart attack, for example, is well known in Western emergency medicine to sometimes cause pain that radiates down the left arm rather than staying in the chest — a connection modern medicine explains through shared nerve pathways. Chinese medicine had already mapped a very similar connection centuries earlier: the Heart meridian runs from the chest down the inner arm, and pain along that exact path has long been read as a sign of a heart-related pattern.": "9edc946496e7",
"One of the clearest ways to make meridians feel less abstract is to connect them to something Western medicine already accepts: referred pain. A heart attack, for example, is well known in Western emergency medicine to sometimes cause pain that radiates down the left arm rather than staying in the chest — a connection modern medicine explains through shared nerve pathways. Chinese medicine had already mapped a very similar connection centuries earlier: the Heart meridian runs from the chest down the inner arm, and pain along that exact path has long been read as a sign of a heart-related pattern. This kind of pattern shows up elsewhere too. Chinese medicine has long used the location of a headache as a diagnostic clue, because different meridians cross different parts of the head. Pain across the forehead is traditionally linked to the Stomach meridian; pain along the temples is linked to the Gallbladder meridian; pain at the very top of the head is linked to the Liver meridian. A patient describing exactly where a headache sits is, without knowing it, giving a meridian-based clue that a practitioner trained in this system knows how to read. None of this claims that a headache location has only one possible cause, or that this reading replaces an actual examination. What it shows is that \"referred pain\" and \"location-specific symptoms\" are not exotic ideas unique to Chinese medicine — they are a shared observation that two very different medical traditions arrived at through different methods, and meridians are simply the map Chinese medicine built to organize that observation systematically.": "2674141f0b55",
